SURVEY OF AMM COMPLEX TYPES FOR ADM-YANG
========================================

SEMANTIC TYPES
--------------


Need to be represented as typedef.


* amm:type -> YANG native "type"


* amm:typedef -> YANG native "typedef"


* amm:ulist
  UL: Uniform List

  Use YANG "leaf-list", but with multiple values allowed. (Maybe w/ "amm:ulist"
  extension to explicitly signal the difference from a leaf-list?)
  * Pro: Mostly standard YANG
  * Con: Different in maybe unexpected ways.

    leaf-list ll {
      amm:ulist;
      ...
    }

    amm:ulist ll {
      ...
    }




grouping my-ulist {
  type amm:int;
}

uses my-ulist;

list my-umap {
  uses my-ulist;
}





* amm:ulist
* amm:dlist
  DL: Diverse List

  Keep definition since it is special.


* amm:umap
  UM: Uniform Map

  Use YANG "list" with the "amm:umap" extension to signal the UM semantic type
  to be used

    list my-umap {
      amm:umap;
      key um-key;
      leaf um-key { type string; }
      leaf um-value { type binary; }
    }


    typedef my-umap2 {
      list my-umap {
        amm:umap;
        key um-key;
        leaf um-key { type string; }
        leaf um-value { type binary; }
      }
    }

    typedef my-umap3 {
      type amm:umap {
        amm:key { type string; };
        amm:value { type amm:ulist/amm:ari/amm:umap; }
      }
    }


* amm:tblt
  TBLT

  Use YANG "list" with the "amm:tblt" extension to signal the TBLT semantic
  type to be used. Use "leaf" for columns.

    // option 1
    list my-t {
      amm:tblt;
      key t-key;
      leaf t-key { type string; }
      leaf t-value { type binary; }
    }

  -> Named elements are AMM objects not AMM values.

    Using YANG "list" and "unique" statements map directly to the semantics
    defined for AMM and ADM-YANG.

    // option 2
    list my-t2 {
      amm:tblt;
      key t2-key;
      unique "t2-binval t2-intval";
      leaf t2-key { type string; }
      leaf t2-binval { type binary; }
      leaf t2-intval { type amm:int; }
    }

    // option 3
    typedef my-tblt3 {
      type amm:tblt {
        key t3-key;
        unique "t2-binval t2-intval";
        leaf t3-key { type string; }
        leaf t3-binval { type binary; }
        leaf t3-intval { type amm:int; }
      }
    }

  -> Option 3 hits the sweet spot of keeping native YANG semantics while also
     mapping the ADM semantics adequately.


* amm:union -> "union"

  Type matching works similarly enough to YANG to just use "union", as defined
  in dtn-amm.


* amm:seq

  Keep definition since it is special.


OBJECT TYPES
------------

* amm:ident
  IDENT: Identity Object

  YANG "identity" with some tunings:

  * "amm:abstract" (boolean) extension to show that the identity is abstract
  * "amm:parameter"




OBJECT LIFECYCLE
----------------

draft-ietf-dtn-amm Section 7.16 Object lifecycle. It would be good to
align with RFC 7950 Section 11 and also
draft-ietf-netmod-yang-module-versioning Section 3.1.1 on "status"
BC/NBC changes.

RFC 7950 Section 11. Updating a Module

   o  A "status" statement may be added, or changed from "current" to
      "deprecated" or "obsolete", or changed from "deprecated" to
      "obsolete".

draft-ietf-netmod-yang-module-versioning
Section 3.1.1. Backwards-compatible rules

   *  A "status" "deprecated" statement MAY be added, or changed from
      "current" to "deprecated", but adding or changing "status" to
      "obsolete" is a non-backwards-compatible change.  See [RFC9907]
      and Section 6.1.1 for guidance.

   *  YANG schema nodes with a "status" "obsolete" substatement MAY be
      removed from published modules, and the removal is classified as a
      backwards-compatible change.  In some circumstances it MAY be
      helpful to retain the obsolete definitions since their identifiers
      may still be referenced by other modules and to ensure that their
      identifiers are not reused with a different meaning.

Also note the following bullet point directly after the two above

   *  A statement that is defined using the YANG "extension" statement
      may be added, removed, or changed.  While extensions may be
      ignored by some clients, other clients may support a given
      extension and thus be affected if a module adds, removes or
      changes how it uses the extension statement.  Therefore, extension
      statement definitions SHOULD specify whether adding, removing, or
      changing statements defined by that extension are considered
      backwards-compatible or non-backwards-compatible.

I have a few modification suggestions:

1) Bullet point 3: Changing status from "deprecated" to "obsolete" is
   defined as a non-backwards-compatible change.

2) Bullet point 3: Mention that status "obsolete" objects might be
   removed, this is a backwards-compatible change.

3) It would be good to define BC/NBC rules for the extensions defined in
   ADM-YANG. (Not presented below, bigger overhaul needed.)


